Myth: In the time before time, there lived a painter named Solveig who captured light itself on canvas. Her paintings glowed with an inner radiance that warmed homes through the harshest winters and lit paths through the darkest nights.

When the Great Frost came, threatening to freeze all warmth from the world, Solveig knew she must act. She gathered her most luminous pigments—crimson reds like dawn's first blush, golden yellows like summer honey, and silver whites like fresh snow under moonlight.

Climbing to the highest peak, Solveig began to paint directly onto the frozen air. Each brushstroke crackled and sparkled, hanging suspended in the frigid atmosphere. She painted for seven days and seven nights, her fingers becoming ice, her breath crystallizing into frost.

As she completed her final stroke, Solveig's body transformed into pure winter wind, and her paintings merged with the sky itself. The colors began to shimmer and shift, creating ever-changing patterns that danced across the heavens.

Now, when the aurora appears, people say it is Solveig still painting—her brushstrokes visible in waves of color that flow across the polar night, each display a new masterpiece, reminding us that art and sacrifice can transform even the coldest darkness into breathtaking wonder.
