Myth: In the age before memory, the world knew only gray sameness. The Dream Keeper, guardian of sleep and visions, watched humans live without wonder or imagination, their minds as blank as unmarked clay.

Determined to awaken their spirits, she collected dreams from every sleeping creature—the soaring flights of eagles, the deep currents known to whales, the patient growth felt by ancient trees. She wove these dreams into a magnificent cloak of infinite colors and patterns.

But when she tried to drape it across humanity, the cloak dissolved like morning mist, and its essence rained down upon the earth. Where each droplet fell, it seeped into different things: some into flowers, giving them their hues; some into birds, granting them their songs; some into water, teaching it to dance and reflect.

The Dream Keeper despaired until she saw children playing. They were gathering the colored flowers, mimicking the bird songs, splashing in the shimmering water. The dreams had not been lost—they had become woven into the world itself.

Now, when we encounter beauty in nature, we are touching fragments of that original dream-cloak, and our own imaginations awaken, proving that inspiration cannot be given—it must be discovered in the world around us.
